---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import { deleteField, deployRisk } from "../../data/demo-answers.ts";

const blocking = deleteField.groups[0]?.items.length ?? 0;
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="What Breaks If You Delete a Salesforce Field?"
  description="Before you delete a Salesforce field, ask what depends on it. Get every flow, formula, roll-up and Apex class that uses it, plus what wasn't checked."
  path="/use-cases/what-breaks-if-you-delete-a-field"
  heading="What breaks if I delete this field?"
  lede="Deleting a field can’t be undone, and the things that depend on it are spread across flows, formulas, code and permissions. Ask before you click Delete, and get the full list with each item named."
  crumb="Delete a field"
  audience="admins, developers and release managers"
  short={`Ask your AI assistant “can I delete this field?”. sf-intelligence checks your org’s metadata and answers yes or no, names every component that depends on the field, and lists the kinds of metadata it couldn’t check. On the demo org, Invoice Amount has ${blocking} blockers. It never deletes anything itself.`}
  answers={[deleteField, deployRisk]}
  answerIntro="Two questions asked of Verdant, the demo org: the field on its own, then the same delete as part of a deployment."
  asks={[
    "Can I delete the Amount field on Invoice?",
    "What breaks if I delete Account.Legacy_Code__c?",
    "Is it safe to remove the Region picklist on Opportunity?",
    "Is this deployment risky? It deletes three fields.",
  ]}
  checks={[
    "<strong>Formulas and roll-ups</strong> that use the field",
    "<strong>Flows, workflow and validation rules</strong> that read or set it",
    "<strong>Apex</strong> that reads or writes it, from the parsed source",
    "<strong>Page layouts and permissions</strong> that include it",
    "<strong>Reports</strong>, when your refresh pulled them (the most-used 500 by default)",
    "<strong>Name-only mentions</strong> in Apex strings and OmniStudio paths, listed separately for you to check",
  ]}
  limits={[
    "Field names put together piece by piece at run time in Apex",
    "Managed-package code, integrations and API loads",
    "Anything changed in the org since your last refresh",
    "Kinds of metadata your refresh didn’t pull. The answer lists them as “not checked”, never “none”",
  ]}
  tools={["safe_to_delete_field", "get_impact", "field_360", "what_if_change_field_type", "review_change", "coverage_report"]}
  related={[
    { href: "/blog/delete-unused-salesforce-fields", label: "Can I delete this Salesforce field?", note: "the full cleanup method" },
    { href: "/use-cases/where-is-a-field-used", label: "Where is a field used?", note: "the list without a verdict" },
    { href: "/use-cases/impact-analysis", label: "Impact analysis", note: "flows, classes and objects too" },
    { href: "/use-cases/consultants", label: "For consultants", note: "pre-deploy reviews on client orgs" },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "Isn’t Salesforce’s “Where is this used?” button enough?",
      a: "It is a good start, but it works one field at a time and misses some references, such as field names in dynamic Apex and some formula and Lightning uses. sf-intelligence answers from the whole metadata snapshot at once and tells you which kinds of metadata it did and didn’t check.",
    },
    {
      q: "Does it delete the field for me?",
      a: "No. sf-intelligence is read-only. It tells you what depends on the field and what to change first. The delete stays your job, in Setup or in a deployment.",
    },
    {
      q: "What does “not checked” mean in an answer?",
      a: "Those kinds of metadata weren’t in your snapshot, so the tool can’t say whether they use the field. It says so instead of implying they don’t. Widen your refresh to include them, or check those by hand.",
    },
  ]}
  cta={{ question: deleteField.question, related: { href: "/blog/delete-unused-salesforce-fields", label: "Read the field cleanup guide" } }}
  dateModified="2026-10-10"
>
  <h2>Why a plain AI assistant gets this wrong</h2>
  <p>Ask a general chatbot whether you can delete a field and it lists the places fields are usually used: page layouts, reports, validation rules. It can’t know which of those exist in your org, so it either guesses or tells you to check everything yourself. sf-intelligence answers from your org’s actual metadata, so the list is your list, with each item named.</p>
  <h2>How to read the answer</h2>
  <p>The answer comes in three parts. <strong>Blocking</strong> items are things Salesforce will refuse the delete over, or that will break straight after it. <strong>What it checked</strong> shows where it looked and found something, where it looked and found nothing, and what it couldn’t look at. A short <strong>next step</strong> tells you what to change first.</p>
  <p>A clean answer means no dependents were found in the kinds of metadata that were checked. That is a narrower claim than “unused”, and the answer is worded that way on purpose.</p>
</UseCaseLayout>
